Forward and backward chaining have different use-cases. Forward chaining starts with the facts and uses them to derive new facts. This process can happen dynamically, and so if we learn something new and add it to the knowledge based, we can use forward chaining to derive new facts without any particular goal in mind. For example, we can derive the following new rules from our rule base

If animal goes “moo” then animal eats grass [rules 1+4];

If animal goes “neigh” then animal eats oats [rules 2+5];

If animal goes “baa” then animal eats grass [rule 3+6].

These can then be added to the knowledge base, which can then grow dynamically as new facts are added. Thus, forward chaining can be used without necessarily having a specific goal/question in mind.

If we have a specific hypothesis that we wish to test then we can of course answer this using forward chaining, but one drawback of this is that since forward chaining starts with the known facts and tests the antecedents of our rules, we do not know whether the desired goal is a possible consequent of any of the rules. Consider what would happen if we wanted to find a medicine that could be absoprbed through the skin. We would have to follow all possible forward paths through the rule base to determine that there was no consequent that would confirm the hypothesis. With backward chaining, which is goal-driven, we test the consequents first, so if “intracutaneous” is not found in the set of possible consequents we know that we can immediately reject the hypothesis. Backward chaining also invokes only those rules that can lead to the desired consequent and thus can be much more efficient (it is not, for the particular example we have used here). With forward chaining, it is possible to head down dead-end pathways. Forwards and Backward Chaining are therefore employed with different tasks in mind (typically open vs closed questions).

What does this give us over what we can already do with propositional logic? Economy of representation and a clear structure. It will be helpful to develop a more extensive example to illustates this. Consider the following set of propositions:

P1: Alice is a Student

P2: Diana is a Professor

P3: Diana teaches ECS8050

P4: Alice is enrolled on the AI Course

P5: The AI Course require module ECS8050

Consider the question: does Diana advise (ie. teach) Alice? We can easily infer that she does by reading the sentences, but to determine this from a purely symbolic representation we would need an explicit rule that expresses this:

P1 \land P2 \land P3 \land P4 \land P5 \implies P6

where P6: “Diana advises Alice”. Given this rule, if all of P1-P5 are true, P6 must also be true (notice that we do not remove the possibility that there is another way in which Diana could advise Alice).

This does the job for this specific case, but we would need a separate rule for every possible case. If we want to determine whether Bob is advised by Faye, this rule will not do this. It will not even work if we change ECS8050 to ECS8051. Even for this very simple problem, we need a large number of rules (that are all very similar) to build anything resembling a functional knowledge base.

This is where first order logic shows its hand. Predicates, variables, and quantifiers allow us to write rules that capture multiple cases. Let’s build an example. we define a set of predicates to model a simple university program management structure:

Predicate

Meaning

\mathrm{Student}(x)

x is a student

\mathrm{Professor}(x)

x is a Professor

\mathrm{Course}(x)

x is a degree Course

\mathrm{Module}(x)

x is a course module

\mathrm{Enrolled}(s,p)

Student s is enrolled on Course p

\mathrm{Requires}(p,m)

Course p requires module m

\mathrm{Teaches}(p,m)

Professor p teaches module m

\mathrm{Passed}(s,m)

Student s passed module m

\mathrm{Advises}(p,s)

Professor p advises student s

\mathrm{Graduates}(s,p)

Student s graduates from course p

Using these predicates we can define some basic facts such as those represented by P1-P5 in our previous examples. These are facts that are known and that are not derived from other facts.

\mathrm{Student(Alice)}

\mathrm{Student(Bob)}

\mathrm{Student(Chen)}

\mathrm{Professor(Diana)}

\mathrm{Professor(Edward)}

\mathrm{Professor(Faye)}

\mathrm{Course(AI)}

\mathrm{Module(ECS8050)}

\mathrm{Module(ECS8051)}

\mathrm{Module(ECS8052)}

\mathrm{Enrolled(Alice,AI)}

\mathrm{Enrolled(Bob,AI)}

\mathrm{Enrolled(Chen,AI)}

\mathrm{Teaches(Diana,ECS8050)}

\mathrm{Teaches(Edward,ECS8051)}

\mathrm{Teaches(Faye,ECS8052)}

\mathrm{Requires(AI,ECS8050)}

\mathrm{Requires(AI,ECS8051)}

\mathrm{Requires(AI,ECS8052)}

\mathrm{Passed(Alice,ECS8050)}

\mathrm{Passed(Alice,ECS8051)}

\mathrm{Passed(Alice,ECS8052)}

\mathrm{Passed(Bob,ECS8050)}

\mathrm{Passed(Bob,ECS8052)}

\mathrm{Passed(Chen,ECS8050)}

\mathrm{Passed(Chen,ECS8051)}

\mathrm{Passed(Chen,ECS8052)}

So far, we have not gained very much. We could have created propositions that represented each of these facts, and we would have required the same number of sentences to express the same information. The difference comes when we want to derive new knowledge from these facts such, for example, whether a Professor advises a particular student. In propositional logic we have to create separate rules for every possible combination. In first order logic, we need just the one rule:\exists m,c [\mathrm{Professor}(p) \land \mathrm{Student}(s) \land \mathrm{Teaches}(p,m) \land \mathrm{Course}(c) \land \mathrm{Enrolled}(s,c) \land \mathrm{Requires}(c,m) \implies \mathrm{Advises}(p,s)]

The “English” translation of this is: if there exist a module m and a course c such that Professor p teaches module m, and module m is required is required on course c and student s is enrolled on course c, them Professor p advises Student s. Notice that the quantifier over m and p is essential here because what we want to determine whether \mathrm{Advises}(c,s), we only need there to be some m and c such that the statement is satisfied.

Knowledge bases rooted in logic provide us with a powerful mechanism for representing and reasoning about our knowledge of the world. It provides us with theoretically sound and robust tools for querying our knowledge. They are readily and easily updated, are human-readable (at least in principle), and, importantly, provides us with mechanisms for deriving new knowledge using techniques such as forward chaining.

However, knowledge bases are not a panacea.

They are difficult to construct, requiring extensive input from subject matter experts to ensure that the logical formulation really does correspond to the knowledge that is being encoded.

They do not scale well, and there are no annotations to support interpretability.

The constraint-based approach to programming is not terribly intuitive for most programmers who are used to imperative, object-oriented, or functional styles of programming.

These limitations make knowledge bases of limited use for large-scale applications. The scaling is the real bottleneck: it is simply not practical to build and maintain large scale systems represented in this way.

Systems based in first order logic do have some desirable properties:

Explicit modelling of relationships between entities.

The notion of “types”: each variable has a domain in which it is valid (for a given predicate) and this permits a basic form of type checking.

Practical, scalable information retrieval systems benefit from having certain additional properties that can require some significant contortions to add to a logic-based knowledge base. These include:

Annotation of entities with additional information.

Scalability to large numbers of entities.

Highly parallel querying.

Online updating.

Readable by machines.

Interpretable by humans.

One type of system designed with these goals in mind is the knowledge graph. Consider the following rule-based system in first order logic:

\mathrm{Student(Alice)}

\mathrm{Student(Bob)}

\mathrm{Student(Chen)}

\mathrm{Professor(Diana)}

\mathrm{Professor(Edward)}

\mathrm{Professor(Faye)}

\mathrm{Course(AI)}

\mathrm{Module(ECS8050)}

\mathrm{Module(ECS8051)}

\mathrm{Module(ECS8052)}

\mathrm{Enrolled(Alice,AI)}

\mathrm{Enrolled(Bob,AI)}

\mathrm{Enrolled(Chen,AI)}

\mathrm{Teaches(Diana,ECS8050)}

\mathrm{Teaches(Edward,ECS8051)}

\mathrm{Teaches(Faye,ECS8052)}

\mathrm{Requires(AI,ECS8050)}

\mathrm{Requires(AI,ECS8051)}

\mathrm{Requires(AI,ECS8052)}

\mathrm{Passed(Alice,ECS8050)}

\mathrm{Passed(Alice,ECS8051)}

\mathrm{Passed(Alice,ECS8052)}

\mathrm{Passed(Bob,ECS8050)}

\mathrm{Passed(Bob,ECS8052)}

\mathrm{Passed(Chen,ECS8050)}

\mathrm{Passed(Chen,ECS8051)}

\mathrm{Passed(Chen,ECS8052)}

It is not difficult to see how this could be drawn as a network, or graph.

Unary Predicates correspond to nodes in the graph

Binary Predicates correspond to directed edges

This is an example of a knowledge graph.
